<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fuad Stephens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Donald Stephens, Tun Fuad Stephens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fuad Stephens, born Donald Stephens, was a journalist and Kadazan leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> who founded the United National Kadazan Organisation. He helped bring the colony into Malaysia in 1963 as the state of Sabah and served as its first chief minister. He became chief minister again in 1976 but died weeks later in an air crash.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fuad-stephens.jpg" alt="Fuad Stephens" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tun_Fuad_Stephens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephens was born Donald Aloysius Marmaduke Stephens at Kudat on 14 September 1920. His father, Jules Stephens, was half Kadazan and half British and had dropped the surname Pavitt of his own father, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. His mother, Edith Cope, was of mixed Japanese, British and Dusun ancestry from Kinabatangan. His younger brother Leo Benedict later became President of the Dewan Negara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Stephens founded the United National Kadazan Organisation in August 1961. At first he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>'s joining a federation, but he came round to it and negotiated the formation of Malaysia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustapha-harun">Tun Mustapha</a> of the United Sabah National Organisation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-kuan-yew">Lee Kuan Yew</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. He saw Malaysia as a federation of four equal partners, wanted English as Sabah's official language and promoted a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadazan-dusun">Kadazan-Dusun</a> identity, without success.</p><p>He was chief minister from 16 September 1963, at the head of the Sabah Alliance. Relations with the federal government soured when he pressed Sabah's rights under the 20-point agreement and sought to replace British officials with local men as fast as they could be trained, while the federal government preferred officials from Peninsular Malaysia. Forced to resign on 31 December 1964, he became the first federal cabinet member from Sabah. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> left Malaysia in 1965 he sought a review of Sabah's place in the federation, which the federal government rejected. He was High Commissioner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1968 to 1973 and governor of Sabah from 1973 to 1975.</p><p>On 7 January 1971 he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with his wife and five children at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustapha-harun">Tun Mustapha</a>'s residence and took the name Muhammad Fuad. In 1975, with Harris Salleh, he founded the Sabah People's United Front (BERJAYA), which defeated Mustapha's party in the 1976 state election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 6 June 1976 his plane from Labuan crashed about 2 kilometres from Kota Kinabalu International Airport, killing everyone on board, among them his son Johari and several cabinet members. The disaster is known as the Double Six Crash, and the Double Six Monument marks the site. He was buried at the State Mausoleum near the Sabah State Mosque and was given the title Bapa Malaysia Dari Sabah, Father of Malaysia from Sabah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fuad Stephens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local political leaders emerged in the early 1960s, among them <b>Donald Stephens</b> of the Kadazan community and Mustapha Harun.</blockquote><a href="/state/crown-colony-of-north-borneo">Crown Colony of North Borneo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kudat, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Kota Kinabalu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.88,116.83&amp;year=1956" aria-label="Kudat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Fuad Stephens"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4731 4413h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4731 4413h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.88,116.83&amp;year=1956">What was here in 1956? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
